.TH QEMU-TUI 1 "November 2026" "qemu-tui 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
qemu-tui \- keyboard-driven terminal interface for managing QEMU virtual machines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qemu-tui
.RB [ \-\-console ]
.RB [ \-\-vm\-dir
.IR DIR ]
.br
.B qemu-tui
.I command
.RI [ argument " ...]"
.br
.B qemu-tui
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B qemu-tui
is a lightweight frontend for QEMU written in Go.
It creates and manages virtual machines stored in a per-user directory,
generates the QEMU command line, starts QEMU directly and controls the running
process through the QEMU Machine Protocol (QMP).
It is not a replacement for QEMU or libvirt and needs no daemon or background service.
Virtual machines keep running when
.B qemu-tui
exits.
.PP
Without arguments the full-screen terminal interface is started.
With a
.I command
argument, one console command (see
.BR "CONSOLE COMMANDS" )
is executed and the program exits, which is convenient for scripts.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-console
Open the interface directly in the global command console.
.TP
.BI \-\-vm\-dir " DIR"
Use
.I DIR
as the virtual machine directory for this run instead of the one from the configuration file.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH THE INTERFACE
.SS Main screen
The main screen lists all virtual machines with their state
(Running, Paused or Stopped), CPU and memory allocation, disk size, network mode and uptime.
Columns are dropped automatically on narrow terminals, and all text is re-wrapped when the
terminal is resized.
.TP
.BR "Up" ", " "Down" ", " j ", " k
Move the selection.
.TP
.B Enter
Open the menu of the selected virtual machine.
.TP
.B /
Search virtual machines by name.
.B Enter
keeps the filter,
.B Esc
clears it.
.TP
.B Ctrl+N
Create a new virtual machine (wizard).
.TP
.BR : " or " Ctrl+G
Open the global console.
.TP
.B t
Open the resource monitor.
.TP
.BR s " or " e
Open the application settings.
.TP
.B Ctrl+R
Refresh the states (they also refresh every two seconds).
.TP
.BR Ctrl+Q " or " Ctrl+C
Quit.
Running virtual machines are not affected.
.PP
The bindings for
.BR quit ,
.BR new ,
.BR refresh ,
.BR console ,
.B search
and
.B top
can be changed with the
.B keybindings
option of the global configuration.
.SS Virtual machine menu
.TP
.B Start
Build the QEMU command, launch QEMU, and open the per-VM console showing the log.
.TP
.B Stop
Send an ACPI power-down request (graceful shutdown).
.TP
.B Force Stop
Tell QEMU to quit immediately.
If QEMU does not answer, the process is killed, but only after verifying that it is the
process started for this virtual machine.
.TP
.B Restart
Reset the guest.
.TP
.BR Pause ", " Resume
Freeze or continue the guest.
.TP
.B Settings
Open the settings screen of the virtual machine.
.TP
.B Console
Open the per-VM console.
.TP
.B Logs
Scrollable log viewer.
.B r
reloads.
.TP
.B Snapshots
List snapshots.
.B n
creates one,
.BR Enter " or " r
restores the selected one,
.B d
deletes it.
.TP
.B Attach Media
Ask for an ISO name or path.
An empty answer ejects the medium.
.TP
.B Clone
Copy a stopped virtual machine (disks, firmware variables, snapshots) under a new name.
The clone gets a new MAC address.
.TP
.B Delete
Remove a stopped virtual machine and all its files.
The name of the machine must be typed to confirm.
.SS Creation wizard
Started with
.B Ctrl+N
or with
.B :create
without arguments.
The steps are: name, architecture
.RB ( x86_64 ,
.BR aarch64 ,
.BR i386 ,
.BR riscv64 ),
RAM in MiB, CPU cores, disk size, disk format
.RB ( qcow2
or
.BR raw ),
firmware
.RB ( bios
or
.BR uefi ),
network
.RB ( user ,
.B tap
or
.BR none ),
TAP interface (only for
.BR tap ),
display
.RB ( gtk ,
.BR sdl ,
.BR vnc ,
.BR spice )
and an optional ISO.
.B Enter
continues,
.B Shift+Tab
goes back,
the arrow keys change a choice and
.B Esc
cancels.
Every answer is validated.
.SS Settings screens
Everyday changes do not require editing files.
Use
.B Up
and
.B Down
to move,
.B Left
and
.B Right
to change choices and toggles (saved immediately), and
.B Enter
to edit text and number fields.
Invalid values are rejected with an error message and are not saved.
.PP
Settings of a virtual machine: memory, CPU cores, display, boot device, firmware, network mode,
TAP interface, disk bus
.RB ( virtio
or
.BR ide ,
the latter for legacy guests), serial console, MAC address and ISO.
They apply at the next start, except the ISO, which is changed live.
.PP
Application settings: default display, theme
.RB ( dark
or
.BR light ),
ISO folder and the editor command.
The last row of both screens opens the raw JSON file in an editor, for advanced changes.
.SH CONSOLE COMMANDS
The global console is opened with
.BR : " or " Ctrl+G .
A per-VM console is opened from the virtual machine menu.
In a per-VM console the
.I vm
argument is implied, so
.B :snapshot clean-install
snapshots that machine.
Keys:
.B Enter
runs,
.B Tab
completes,
.B Up
and
.B Down
browse the history (kept in the
.I history
file),
.B PgUp
and
.B PgDn
scroll,
.B Esc
closes.
.PP
Arguments are split using simple quoting rules
(double quotes, single quotes and backslash) but are never passed to a shell.
A leading colon is optional.
.TP
.B help
List the commands.
.TP
.BR list " [" snapshots ]
List virtual machines, or the snapshots of a machine.
.TP
.BR start " \fIvm\fR"
Start a virtual machine.
.TP
.BR stop " \fIvm\fR"
Request an ACPI power-down.
.TP
.BR restart " \fIvm\fR"
Reset the guest.
.TP
.BR kill " \fIvm\fR"
Force stop.
.TP
.BR pause " \fIvm\fR, " resume " \fIvm\fR"
Freeze or continue the guest.
.TP
.BR status " [\fIvm\fR]"
Without argument, the same as
.BR list .
With a machine: state, PID, runtime, CPU, memory, disk I/O and network information.
.TP
.B top
Open the resource monitor.
.TP
.BR create " \fIname\fR [\fIkey\fR=\fIvalue\fR ...]"
Create a virtual machine.
Without arguments the wizard opens.
Keys:
.BR mem " (" memory ", " ram "),"
.BR cpus " (" cpu "),"
.BR arch ,
.B disk
(size),
.BR fmt " (" format "),"
.BR fw " (" firmware "),"
.BR net " (" network "),"
.BR tap ,
.BR display ,
.B iso
and
.BR boot .
.TP
.BR delete " \fIvm\fR"
Delete a stopped virtual machine and its files.
.TP
.BR clone " \fIvm\fR \fInew\fR"
Clone a stopped virtual machine.
.TP
.BR snapshot " \fIvm\fR \fIname\fR"
Create an internal qcow2 snapshot of the main disk.
The machine must be stopped.
.TP
.BR restore " \fIvm\fR \fIname\fR"
Restore a snapshot.
The machine must be stopped.
.TP
.BR snapshots " \fIvm\fR"
List snapshots with their creation dates.
.TP
.BR logs " [\fIvm\fR]"
Show recent log lines of a machine, or the global event log.
.TP
.BR usb " attach|remove \fIvm\fR \fIvvvv\fR:\fIpppp\fR"
Attach or detach a host USB device by vendor and product id (see
.BR lsusb (8)).
A running machine is changed live.
.TP
.BR disk " info \fIvm\fR"
Show format, virtual size, size on disk and snapshot count of each disk.
.TP
.BR disk " resize \fIvm\fR \fIsize\fR"
Grow the main disk, for example
.B 40G
or
.BR +10G .
Shrinking is not supported.
The machine must be stopped.
.TP
.BR disk " create \fIvm\fR \fIfile\fR \fIsize\fR [qcow2|raw]"
Create an extra disk in the machine's
.I disks
directory and attach it.
.TP
.BR disk " attach|detach \fIvm\fR \fIfile\fR"
Attach an existing file from the
.I disks
directory, or detach it (the file is kept).
.TP
.BR iso " list"
List the ISO files of the ISO folder.
.TP
.BR iso " attach \fIvm\fR \fIfile\fR"
Insert an ISO.
A relative name is searched in the ISO folder, then in the machine directory.
Works on running machines.
.TP
.BR iso " eject \fIvm\fR"
Remove the medium.
.TP
.BR boot " \fIvm\fR disk|cdrom|network"
Select the boot device for the next start.
The order is disk, CD, network for
.BR disk ;
CD, disk, network for
.BR cdrom ;
and network, CD, disk for
.BR network .
The boot menu is applied on x86 guests; other architectures follow the order of their firmware.
.TP
.BR monitor " \fIvm\fR \fIcommand\fR"
Run a QEMU monitor command through QMP, for example
.BR "info status" ,
.B info block
or
.BR "info network" .
.TP
.BR serial " [\fIvm\fR]"
Print the path of the serial console socket and a command to connect to it
(see
.BR socat (1)).
The serial port must be enabled in the settings of the machine.
.TP
.B clear
Clear the console screen (interface only).
.TP
.BR exit ", " quit
Leave the console.
.SH RESOURCE MONITOR
Opened with
.B t
or
.BR :top .
It refreshes every second and shows, for each running virtual machine, CPU load (relative to
its core count), resident memory compared to the configured memory, and disk read and write rates.
Disk rates are available on Linux only.
On other systems CPU and memory come from
.BR ps (1)
and CPU is a decayed average.
.SH VIRTUAL MACHINE CONFIGURATION
Each machine has a
.I config.json
file.
Only
.B name
is required, everything else has a default.
.TP
.B name
Machine name.
Letters, digits and
.BR . ,
.B _
and
.BR \- ;
at most 63 characters; must match the directory name.
.TP
.B memory
RAM in MiB (default 2048).
.TP
.B cpus
Number of virtual CPUs (default 2).
.TP
.B architecture
.BR x86_64 " (default), " aarch64 ", " i386 " or " riscv64 .
.TP
.BR disk ", " disk_format ", " disk_size ", " disk_bus
Main disk file name inside
.IR disks/ ,
its format
.RB ( qcow2
or
.BR raw ),
the requested size, and the bus
.RB ( virtio
or, on x86,
.BR ide ).
.TP
.B extra_disks
List of additional disk file names inside
.IR disks/ .
.TP
.B iso
ISO file name or absolute path.
.TP
.B firmware
.B bios
(x86 only) or
.BR uefi .
Non-x86 guests always use UEFI.
Each machine has its own variable store in
.IR firmware/vars.fd .
.TP
.BR network ", " network_mode ", " tap_iface ", " mac
.B network
is a boolean shortcut;
.B network_mode
is
.BR user ,
.B tap
or
.B none
and takes precedence.
.B tap_iface
names an existing host tap interface.
.B mac
defaults to a generated
.B 52:54:00:xx:xx:xx
address.
.TP
.B display
.BR gtk ", " sdl ", " vnc " or " spice .
.B vnc
listens on 127.0.0.1 starting at port 5900;
.B spice
on 127.0.0.1 starting at port 5930.
Both are unauthenticated and local only.
.TP
.B boot
.BR disk " (default), " cdrom " or " network .
.TP
.B serial
Boolean; enable a serial port reachable through a Unix socket.
.TP
.B usb
List of
.I vvvv:pppp
host USB device ids.
.SH GLOBAL CONFIGURATION
The file
.I config.json
in the configuration directory may contain:
.TP
.B default_display
Display backend for new machines.
.TP
.BR vm_dir ", " log_dir ", " iso_dir
Folders for virtual machines, logs and ISO files.
A leading
.B ~/
is expanded.
.TP
.B theme
.B dark
or
.BR light .
.TP
.B colors
Object overriding the keys
.BR accent ,
.BR dim ,
.BR ok ,
.BR warn ,
.B err
and
.BR text .
Values are ANSI colour numbers or
.BR #rrggbb .
.TP
.B keybindings
Object remapping
.BR quit ,
.BR new ,
.BR refresh ,
.BR console ,
.B search
and
.BR top .
.TP
.B editor
Command used by the "Edit raw config file" rows.
Defaults to the first of micro, nano, ee and pico found in
.BR PATH ,
then
.B $VISUAL
or
.BR $EDITOR ,
then
.BR vi .
.PP
Missing keys fall back to their defaults.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/qemu-tui/config.json
Global configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/qemu-tui/vms/NAME/
Directory of one machine, containing
.IR config.json ,
.IR disks/ ,
.IR firmware/ ,
.IR snapshots/ ,
.I logs/
and, while the machine runs,
.IR runtime.json .
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/qemu-tui/vms/NAME/logs/qemu.log
QEMU output and timestamped events of one machine.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/qemu-tui/logs/events.log
Events of all machines: start, stop, exit status, clean-ups.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/qemu-tui/isos/
Default ISO folder.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/qemu-tui/history
Console command history (last 500 commands).
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/qemu-tui/
QMP and serial sockets (directory mode 0700).
Without
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
.IR /tmp/qemu-tui-UID/ " is used."
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Replaces
.I ~/.config
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Replaces
.I ~/.local/share
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Location of the socket directory.
.TP
.BR VISUAL ", " EDITOR
Fallback editor, see
.BR editor .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred; the message is written to standard error.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command line options.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start the interface:
.PP
.RS
.nf
qemu-tui
.fi
.RE
.PP
Create a UEFI machine with an installer ISO from the ISO folder, then start it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
qemu-tui create debian mem=4096 cpus=4 disk=40G fw=uefi iso=debian.iso
qemu-tui start debian
.fi
.RE
.PP
Take and restore a snapshot:
.PP
.RS
.nf
qemu-tui stop debian
qemu-tui snapshot debian clean-install
qemu-tui restore debian clean-install
.fi
.RE
.PP
Query a running guest through the monitor:
.PP
.RS
.nf
qemu-tui monitor debian "info status"
.fi
.RE
.SH SECURITY
QEMU is started with an argument vector, never through a shell, and no value from a
configuration file is interpreted by a shell.
Machine names must consist of letters, digits and
.BR . ,
.BR _ ,
.BR \- ;
disk and ISO names must be plain file names; paths are joined so that they cannot leave their
directory; commas in paths are escaped for QEMU.
.PP
A process id is signalled only after checking that the command line of that process contains the
private QMP socket of the machine, so a recycled process id or a foreign process is never
killed.
Stale runtime state is removed automatically, and starting a machine twice is refused.
.SH NOTES
Accelerator selection is automatic: KVM on Linux when
.I /dev/kvm
is usable, HVF on macOS, WHPX on Windows, otherwise TCG software emulation.
Guests of a foreign architecture, and all guests on FreeBSD, run with TCG and are slower.
.PP
Platforms: Linux and FreeBSD are the primary targets; macOS is expected to work;
Windows support is experimental.
.SH BUGS
Snapshots are internal qcow2 snapshots of the main disk and need the machine stopped.
Adding, removing and growing disks also need the machine stopped.
The network card model is fixed to virtio.
There are no network byte counters.
Disk I/O rates exist on Linux only.
The per-VM console shows the QEMU log and accepts qemu-tui commands; it is not a terminal into the
guest (use the QEMU window, VNC, SPICE or the serial console).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR qemu-system-x86_64 (1),
.BR qemu-img (1),
.BR socat (1),
.BR lsusb (8)
.PP
Project documentation: the README file shipped with the source.
